  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1231 AM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nSunday...Upper low center over the Pacific NW starts to dig \nsouth across southern OR, sweeping a shortwave across the Great \nBasin through the day. This upper disturbance will tap into the \navailable moisture and kick off convection across the Great \nBasin, in essence robbing much of precip potential from \nColorado. There will still be some afternoon and evening showers\nand thunderstorms for the forecast area, but models are really \nbacking off from earlier more aggressive solutions. Southwest \nflow aloft will still be in place, so expect another very hot \nday with above normal temps, with highs in the 80s for the high \nvalleys, and 90s across the plains.\n\nMonday...Upper low center dumbbells to the northeast across ID and \nMT on Monday, sweeping another shortwave across the region. This \ntime, this feature should be moving across CO and the Rockies, \ntapping into the moisture and providing for more afternoon and \nevening convective activity. Southwest flow aloft continues, so plan \non one more day of hot, above normal temps.\n\nTuesday through Friday...Models are having a hard time deciphering \nwhat will happen with the Pacific trough, but one thing they agree \nupon is shifting the upper ridge of high pressure currently over \ncentral and SE US back to the west across CO. The ridge flattens and \nmoves overhead, effectively shutting down the strong southwest flow \nthat has kept the area very hot, and forecast high temps slowly drop \na few degrees day to day, back down to seasonal normals. Moisture \ntrapped under the ridge will provide the fuel for daily showers and \nthunderstorms, but confidence is rather low on how widespread \nactivity will be. However, area burn scars will still need to be \nmonitored because steering flow aloft will be much lighter, leading \nto slower moving storms and a greater potential for increased storm \ntotal precip. Moore",
